// Pull request author: frack113
// Change: update to existing rule
// Title: Suspect Svchost Activity
// Sigma ID: 16c37b52-b141-42a5-a3ea-bbe098444397
// Status: test | Level: high
// Author: David Burkett, @signalblur
// Date: 2019-12-28 | Modified: 2022-06-27
// ATT&CK: privilege-escalation, stealth, T1055
// Description: It is extremely abnormal for svchost.exe to spawn without any CLI arguments and is normally
//   observed when a malicious process spawns the process and injects code into the process memory
//   space.
// False positives:
//   - Rpcnet.exe / rpcnetp.exe which is a lojack style software. https://www.blackhat.com/docs/us-14/materials/us-14-Kamlyuk-Kamluk-Computrace-Backdoor-Revisited.pdf
// References:
//   - https://web.archive.org/web/20180718061628/https://securitybytes.io/blue-team-fundamentals-part-two-windows-processes-759fe15965e2
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/ed4273b30b6bba81f525a9ba136c2bcdb6d8ca95/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_svchost_execution_with_no_cli_flags.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where (ProcessCommandLine endswith "svchost.exe" and FolderPath endswith "\\svchost.exe") and (not(((InitiatingProcessFolderPath endswith "\\rpcnet.exe" or InitiatingProcessFolderPath endswith "\\rpcnetp.exe") or isnull(ProcessCommandLine))))
